WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
In 2015, Volkswagen covered up a scandal that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.711
affected millions of cars and&nbsp;
cost the company $30 billion.

00:00:07.711 --> 00:00:10.590
There’s never been a better time&nbsp;
to buy a Jetta TDI Clean Diesel.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:14.899
It started when the German automaker began a clean diesel marketing push

00:00:14.899 --> 00:00:18.099
which suggested their cars were
environmentally friendly.

00:00:18.099 --> 00:00:21.720
See how clean that is? It’s not dirty&nbsp;
but you still have a dirty mind.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.320
Turns out “clean” diesel was just a dirty lie.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.960
The automaker claimed it used a system that&nbsp;
helped convert harmful nitrogen oxides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.100
a common type of air pollutant, into harmless&nbsp;
nitrogen and oxygen. (SCR system called AdBlue)

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:40.620
NOx are a family of gases that can cause&nbsp;
asthma, bronchitis, and contributes to smog.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:44.940
The U.S. Environmental Protection&nbsp;
Agency required new diesel vehicles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.440
to emit no more than 0.07 grams of NOx per mile.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.080
Volkswagen claimed it emitted no more&nbsp;
than 0.04 grams of NOx per mile…so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.180
it was quite a shock when three students&nbsp;
at West Virginia University tested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.320
two Volkswagen diesel cars and found&nbsp;
their emissions were off the charts.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.980
They polluted way over the limit on&nbsp;
all of the five routes they drove&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.860
in and around San Francisco, San Diego, and LA.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.940
According to their study, the&nbsp;
NOx emissions for Vehicle A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:20.700
the Volkswagen Jetta, were 15 to 35&nbsp;
times higher than the EPA’s standards.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.860
Those for Vehicle B, the Volkswagen&nbsp;
Passat, were 5 to 20 times higher.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.940
The car models were kept anonymous in the&nbsp;
paper, but we now know their identities. 
&nbsp;

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:35.580
This chart is especially revealing. The&nbsp;
green line way at the bottom reflects the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:40.620
EPA emissions standards. The two Volkswagens&nbsp;
went wayyyy over the allowed emissions.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:47.820
Emissions for the third vehicle they tested, a&nbsp;
BMW X5, crept up only during the hilly drive.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.280
The scientists thought to themselves: were they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.180
screwing up the tests? No, they couldn’t&nbsp;
be. They had driven the cars numerous times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:00.120
They began to suspect something that they didn’t&nbsp;
dare say out loud: Volkswagen was cheating.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.140
It takes a lot of guts to accuse&nbsp;
a multi-billion dollar company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.200
the second-largest automaker&nbsp;
in the world, of cheating.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.020
But that’s exactly what Volkswagen was doing.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:15.660
The non-profit International Council on Clean&nbsp;
Transportation, which commissioned the study by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.280
West Virginia University, turned its findings&nbsp;
over to the Environmental Protection Agency.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.640
The EPA later revealed Volkswagens were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.420
emitting up to 40 times more&nbsp;
pollutants than allowed by law.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.240
Not only did Volkswagen deceive regulators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.100
it deceived consumers who believed they were&nbsp;
purchasing environmentally friendly cars.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.340
So, how did Volkswagen manage to mislead everyone?

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.060
It had installed software that could detect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.020
whether the car was being driven on&nbsp;
the road or tested in a laboratory.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:50.340
When it noticed it was being tested&nbsp;
in a lab, the software told the car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.540
to “behave” by activating controls&nbsp;
to temporarily reduce emissions.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:58.260
It knew it was being tested in a&nbsp;
lab when the car acted differently.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:03.060
Anna Stefanopoulou, a Professor of Mechanical&nbsp;
Engineering at the University of Michigan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:09.180
explained to Wired “computer sensors monitored the&nbsp;
steering column. Under normal driving conditions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:15.120
the column oscillates as the driver negotiates&nbsp;
turns. But during emissions testing, the wheels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.980
of the car move, but the steering wheel doesn't.&nbsp;
That seems to have been the signal for the “defeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.500
device” to turn the catalytic scrubber up to&nbsp;
full power, allowing the car to pass the test.”

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.160
A catalytic scrubber is a device&nbsp;
installed in the exhaust system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:33.780
of a vehicle to reduce the emission of&nbsp;
harmful pollutants like nitrogen oxide.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:38.220
Once the car was back on the road, the&nbsp;
deceptive software would switch off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.920
allowing it to again exceed the standards.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:46.500
When the researchers at West Virginia University&nbsp;
drove the cars on the road, they emitted far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:51.060
more pollutants than when they tested the&nbsp;
same vehicles in a California laboratory.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:55.440
In the lab - emissions were below&nbsp;
EPA regulations, shown in green.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.580
The scientists did not explicitly&nbsp;
accuse Volkswagen of deception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.420
but it was clear when you read&nbsp;
between the lines: “ ...Vehicles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:08.520
A and B were operating as intended&nbsp;
and did not have any malfunctions.”

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.920
In other words, this was no mistake.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.700
The cars were operating exactly as VW intended.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.380
Okay, so when they publish their study&nbsp;
in 2014, Volkswagen is freaking out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.520
But executives choose not to come&nbsp;
clean or admit to any wrongdoing.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:27.360
Even though the California Air Resources&nbsp;
Board (CARB), which oversees air pollution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:31.200
control efforts in the state, grew&nbsp;
suspicious and did its own tests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.740
Volkswagen argued that the&nbsp;
regulator’s testing was flawed.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.120
That the outside air pressure affected the&nbsp;
results. That the routes were inconsistent.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:42.120
Nevertheless, Volkswagen said it would update&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:46.020
the engine software for diesel&nbsp;
cars starting with 2009 models.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.400
You might think Volkswagen was trying to fix&nbsp;
the problem of excess emissions. But actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.140
the recall enhanced the ability of the software&nbsp;
to detect when the car was being tested!

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:59.820
In other words, Volkswagen used the&nbsp;
recall so that it could cheat even better.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.500
After the software update, the&nbsp;
cars did emit less than they had,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:07.200
but they were still not within legal limits.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.380
The California regulator&nbsp;
grew even more suspicious.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:15.060
It noticed emissions of nitrogen&nbsp;
oxides rose after 23 minutes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:19.680
driving…which happened to be one minute&nbsp;
after the end of a standard test cycle!

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:24.660
Volkswagen was cornered and couldn’t&nbsp;
come up with any more excuses.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:30.720
In September 2015, it finally admitted&nbsp;
to regulators that its diesel cars were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.540
equipped with software to cheat emissions testing.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.060
It was later revealed that Volkswagen had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:39.480
aware of the cheating for years&nbsp;
but had chosen to keep it secret.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:43.440
The U.S. Securities and Exchange&nbsp;
Commission claimed top executives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.800
knew about the fraud as early as 2007.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.060
At the time, Volkswagen’s market&nbsp;
share in the U.S. was a mere 1%.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:56.760
It was trying to do what it could to compete with&nbsp;
Toyota to become the world’s largest automaker.

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:01.380
Yet it knew its engines couldn’t meet&nbsp;
America’s stricter emissions standards.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.680
There were also allegations of&nbsp;
cheating in emissions testing by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.740
other car companies but none to the same extent.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:13.560
Volkswagen admitted that the software was&nbsp;
installed on 11 million of its cars - most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:17.220
of them in Europe, where diesel-powered&nbsp;
vehicles were popular at the time.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:24.000
The company recalled all 11 million vehicles to&nbsp;
remove the deceptive software and also to retrofit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.440
the cars to meet emissions regulations&nbsp;
- a time-consuming and costly endeavor.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:32.760
But the biggest expense came when&nbsp;
Volkswagen had to pay about $30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.720
billion in fines and settlements,&nbsp;
which is far more than it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.620
have cost to install adequate pollution&nbsp;
control equipment in the first place.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.100
Several top executives at Volkswagen resigned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:49.560
including CEO Martin Winterkorn who&nbsp;
maintained he was unaware of any wrongdoing.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:54.660
The company insisted that the illegal software&nbsp;
was just the work of a couple of rogue engineers.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:00.480
In the month following “diesel gate,” Volkswagen's&nbsp;
worldwide sales fell 5%. (October 2015)

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.240
There was even speculation&nbsp;
that Volkswagen was finished.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:08.700
Yet, Volkswagen was able to weather&nbsp;
the storm and has largely recovered.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:13.500
In 2022, it was the second best-selling&nbsp;
automaker in the world behind Toyota.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:19.380
The company regained consumer trust in part&nbsp;
by introducing electric and hybrid vehicles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:22.320
advancing its commitment to&nbsp;
sustainable transportation.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:27.480
It is determined to sell&nbsp;
more EVs than Tesla by 2025.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:31.560
But there’s a long road ahead as&nbsp;
automakers have lost billions.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:33.900
This doesn't just affect you trying to buy a car;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:37.980
the everyday investor is getting&nbsp;
whacked by the tremendous price fall.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:43.140
Because the S&amp;P 500 is the most popular&nbsp;
index used by the public to build their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:47.640
personal wealth, and it includes A LOT of&nbsp;
exposure to automakers and their stock.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:55.320
But there is an asset class that has outpaced&nbsp;
the S&amp;P by more than 130% over the last 26 years.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.840
I’m talking about contemporary art.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.040
The art market used to be impossible&nbsp;
to access unless you were super rich.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:06.840
With Masterworks, everyone can enter the&nbsp;
high-end art market without spending millions.

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:12.600
You can invest in multi-million dollar&nbsp;
artworks by Banksy, Basquiat, and Monet.

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:15.540
All of the offerings have&nbsp;
been qualified by the SEC

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:20.580
And every painting Masterworks has sold to&nbsp;
date has returned a profit to investors.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.940
The results from these eleven&nbsp;
paintings speak for themselves.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:28.200
652,000 people have signed up for Masterworks.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.000
There’s a waitlist for new users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.080
but you can skip the waitlist by clicking&nbsp;
on my custom link in the description.

